# Solution: Handling 429s from a 10 TPS downstream

## 1. The problem in simple words

Your pipeline has **no speed control**. Kafka hands records to the consumers as fast as they can take them, but the slowest downstream API accepts only **10 requests per second**.

| Item | Value |
|---|---|
| Pods × records per poll | 3 × 50 = **150 records in flight at once** |
| Downstream limit (slowest plan) | **10 tps** |
| Result | Bursts of up to 150 calls hit a 10 tps door, so most get **429** |

The overall speed of the pipeline is decided by the **slowest downstream plan**, not by the number of pods. Adding pods would only make the 429s worse.

### Current flow (what goes wrong)

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    EXT["External app<br/>100,000 requests"] -->|REST| EFA["External facing app"]
    EFA -->|"1 event per request"| K[("Kafka topic")]
    K -->|"poll 50"| P1["Subscriber pod 1"]
    K -->|"poll 50"| P2["Subscriber pod 2"]
    K -->|"poll 50"| P3["Subscriber pod 3"]
    P1 -->|"blocking REST"| ORCH["Domain orchestrator"]
    P2 -->|"blocking REST"| ORCH
    P3 -->|"blocking REST"| ORCH
    ORCH -->|"burst, no limiter"| D1["Downstream API<br/>10 tps plan"]
    D1 -. "HTTP 429" .-> ORCH
    ORCH -. "failure" .-> P1

    style D1 fill:#ffd6d6,stroke:#c00
```

### The math you must accept

Whatever you build, the 10 tps limit is a hard ceiling:

- 10,000 requests per batch ÷ 10 tps = about **17 minutes**
- 100,000 requests ÷ 10 tps = about **2 hours 47 minutes**

Aim for about **8 tps** (80% of the limit) as your safe target. That leaves headroom for retries, and the 100k batch then takes about 3.5 hours. Tell the external app owners the realistic completion time. This is a throughput limit, not a bug.

